{"chunk_id": "058b2d45e7f5", "text": "In this chapter of the Nala\u2013Damayanti tale, the ruined king Nala, stripped of kingdom and possessions by fate and the influence of Kali, wanders the forest with his devoted wife. After birds carry away his garment, he points out the roads that could lead Damayanti back to her father\u2019s land, subtly urging her to leave him for safety. Damayanti refuses, insisting that a wife is the greatest comfort in suffering and that she will not abandon him. Though he professes love, Nala becomes tormented by the thought that she would suffer less without him. While she sleeps in a travelers\u2019 shed, he debates whether to leave. Overcome by despair and Kali\u2019s influence, he cuts their shared garment in half and secretly abandons her, repeatedly returning in anguish before finally departing.\nTormented by misfortune and Kali\u2019s influence, Nala secretly cuts their shared cloth and abandons the sleeping Damayanti in the forest.\nThemes: marital devotion, despair and exile, moral conflict, fate and Kali's influence, abandonment", "vector": [0.003378497436642647, -0.028513632714748383, 0.0006946960929781199, -0.015946917235851288, -0.014965567737817764, 0.02886800840497017, -0.024152083322405815, -0.0041843620128929615, -0.020213058218359947, -0.020458394661545753, 0.006276542786508799, 0.026782643049955368, 0.01905452087521553, 0.02097632922232151, 0.0067331427708268166, 0.013888810761272907, 0.0487675815820694, -0.017119083553552628, 0.023347921669483185, -0.0225710216909647, -0.001538468524813652, 0.00909792073071003, -0.0005571198416873813, -0.00042657149606384337, -0.017010044306516647, -0.0005311379791237414, 0.02775036171078682, -0.03309325873851776, 0.02322525344789028, -0.008130202069878578, -0.00607209512963891, -0.004378587007522583, -0.002209738362580538, -0.03222094848752022, -0.0373457707464695, -0.017800575122237206, 0.006637733895331621, -0.01819584146142006, 0.02075825072824955, -0.009970230050384998, 0.0063446